<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atisha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/982">982</a>–<a href="/years/1054">1054</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Atiśa, Atisha Dipankara Shrijnana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Atisha Dipankara Shrijnana was a Buddhist teacher from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> who taught at the monasteries of the Pala Empire, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramashila">Vikramashila</a>. Invited by the rulers of Guge, he arrived in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in 1042 and spent the rest of his life in Tibet, writing the Lamp for the Path to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. His disciples founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadam-school">Kadam school</a>, and his work was central to the revival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in Tibet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atisha.jpg" alt="Atisha" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown不具名 [Tibet (a Kadampa monastery)] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atisha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scholars trained in Pala monasteries carried Indian Buddhist texts and practices abroad, and the most celebrated of them, <b>Atisha Dipankara Shrijnana</b>, left Vikramashila around 1040 and spent his last years in Tibet, where his teaching helped shape the later spread of Buddhism and the Kadam school.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>They founded Tholing monastery in 996, sent the translator Rinchen Zangpo to Kashmir and invited the Indian teacher <b>Atisha</b>, who arrived in 1042, beginning the later diffusion of Buddhism in Tibet.</blockquote><a href="/state/guge">Guge</a></li></ul></section>
